Own project with help of AI-agent


Start the Fishing Guide on Windows
==================================

1. Install JDK 21 or later.
2. Double-click start-app.bat in the project folder.
3. Wait for the app to start; your browser opens automatically. The startup
   script uses port 8080, or selects an available port up to 8090 if it is
   already in use.

A console window displays the server logs and must remain open while the app
is running. Close the window to stop the server.

You can also start the app manually from the project folder:

    .\mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run

Then open http://localhost:8080/ in your browser. Do not open
src\main\resources\static\index.html directly from the file system: the page
needs the Spring Boot server for API requests, including /api/evaluate.

Log catches with the "Log catch" button. Choose a species from the list or
enter a custom name; weight and length are optional. To set the catch
location, click "Choose location on map" and then click the map. The catch log
is stored locally in your browser and is not shared between browsers or
devices.





FEATURES
1. A basic page with a species dropdown and a Leaflet map (OpenStreetMap).
   The initial map position is near Vaxholm in the Stockholm archipelago.
   Clicking the map saves the latitude and longitude and displays a marker.
2. Fetch weather for the selected location from Open-Meteo (no API key
   required): wind speed (m/s), wind direction (degrees), air pressure, cloud
   cover, temperature, and a 48-hour hourly forecast. Display the data in a
   simple table and handle API errors.
3. Use the rule engine from fishing-rules.js (provided). Do NOT change the
   rules: call evaluate(species, conditions) and display score, rating,
   reasons, methods, and warnings. Calculate pressureTrend as the difference
   in air pressure between now and three hours earlier, and calculate
   isDawnOrDusk based on sunrise/sunset (±1 hour) from Open-Meteo.
4. Display a timeline with hourly scores for the next 24–48 hours to identify
   the best fishing window. Calculate each hourly score with the rule engine
   and highlight the best consecutive three-hour window.
5. Add a list of custom spots (JSON file) with these fields: name, lat, lng,
   species, bottom type, and wind shelter (the wind directions the spot is
   sheltered from). Mark the spots on the map and sort them by how well they
   suit the current wind direction.
6. Log catches with a species, optional weight and length, and an optional
   location marker on the map. Store the log locally in the browser.

